# sbt 2's runner starts a background server and talks to it through a native client unless told
# otherwise. On a machine that caps concurrent builds, a server that outlives its command is a JVM
# nobody queued for, and a client joins every argument into one command line. `--server` runs sbt
# in the foreground, as sbt 1 did, for every invocation that starts here: people, tools and CI.
--server
# The launcher's heap, which since the tests left it on 2026-09-25 is the compiler's and nothing
# else's. 1024 MB, the runner's default, stopped holding the instrumented compile of `theories` on
# 2026-10-21: the coverage job on CI died of `OutOfMemoryError` in it, and here the same compile
# spent 86 to 89% of its time collecting. Measured on 2026-10-22 under a 3 GB ceiling with a GC log,
# `clean coverage theories/compile` keeps 1178 MB live after a young or mixed collection, so 2048
# leaves about 850 MB, and the next time it stops holding the cause is the module's size, which is
# a question about splitting `theories` rather than about this number.
#
# `--mem` and not `-J-Xmx2g`: the runner drops ALL its memory defaults when it sees any `-Xmx`,
# `-Xms` or `-Xss`, so `-J-Xmx2g` alone runs the compiler without `-Xss4M`. `--mem` moves the heap
# and keeps the rest.
--mem 2048
